<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cairo Geniza</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> Geniza is a collection of some 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> fragments kept in the storeroom (geniza) of the Ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> Synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, Old Cairo, where worn writings bearing the name of God were laid aside rather than destroyed. Its letters, contracts and accounts, mostly from the tenth to the thirteenth centuries, are a major source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> and Jewish life in the medieval Islamic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cairo-geniza.jpg" alt="Cairo Geniza" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Kahle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bab_voc_fragment_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In the 1890s the geniza was a room without windows or door, reached only by climbing a ladder to an opening in the wall from the galleries of the Ben Ezra Synagogue. Manuscripts and printed books had been thrown into it over the centuries, and by then it was full of dust and insects. Some papers had been stolen, most likely by the synagogue beadles, and sold to tourists as souvenirs. The first European to note the collection was apparently Simon van Gelderen, who reported on it in 1752 or 1753, and the traveller Jacob Saphir explored it for two days in 1864.</p><p>In 1896 the Scottish twin sisters Agnes S. Lewis and Margaret D. Gibson brought fragments back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and showed them to Solomon Schechter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, who recognised their importance at once. With funds from his colleague <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-taylor-liberia">Charles Taylor</a>, Schechter travelled to Egypt and, with the help of the Chief Rabbi, removed the greater part of the contents of the chamber. Count Riamo d'Hulst later led an excavation of the synagogue courtyard, where documents lay a metre deep, and by May 1898 he had sent 16 large sacks to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The texts are written mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> on vellum and paper, and also on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and cloth. Among the literary fragments, liturgical texts, Biblical and related texts and Rabbinic literature are the commonest, with others on philosophy, science, mysticism and language. Schechter found several incomplete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the original Hebrew of Sirach and two fragments of the Damascus Document. More than 2,500 fragments concern medicine, including prescriptions, druggists' notes and physicians' notebooks.</p><p>The non-literary material includes legal documents, private letters, school exercises, merchants' account books and communal records. Shelomo Dov Goitein estimated about 10,000 items of some length, of which 7,000 were self-contained documents of historical value. His index covers about 35,000 individuals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, and mentions of 450 professions and 450 goods. Places named range from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The payments to labourers form the largest collection of records of day wages in the Islamic world for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, and Jewish bankers' accounts of the 11th century use double-entry bookkeeping.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The fragments are now dispersed. The Taylor-Schechter collection at Cambridge University Library is the largest, with nearly 193,000 fragments. The Jewish Theological Seminary holds a further 43,000, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a> 25,000 folios and the John Rylands Library over 11,000. Pages of a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> are often housed in three or four different libraries. Projects such as the Princeton Geniza Project and a Zooniverse citizen-science project sort and transcribe the digitised fragments. Amitav Ghosh drew on the papers of the merchant Abraham Ben Yiju in In an Antique Land.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cairo Geniza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zurayids profited from Aden's role as the main port between Egypt and India; the documents of the <b>Cairo Geniza</b> describe Jewish and Muslim merchants trading pepper, textiles and metals through the harbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/zurayid-dynasty">Zurayid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
